['replacement_tokens']):\n            raise ValueError(\"replacement_tokens must be list of strings\")\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\n    selected = []\n    intervals = []\n    for c in sorted_calls:\n        overlap = any(c['start'] < e and s < c['end'] for s, e in intervals)\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(c)\n            intervals.append((c['start'], c['end']))\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: (c['start'], c['call_id']))\n    selected_call_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x['start']):\n        start = c['start']\n        end = c['end']\n        repl = c['replacement_tokens']\n        result_tokens = result_tokens[:start] + repl + result_tokens[end:]\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nNow, escape for JSON.\n\nWe need to handle the indentation spaces (4 spaces per level). We'll keep them as spaces, but in JSON string we can embed them directly as spaces with \\n for newlines.\n\nI'll manually escape:\n\n- Replace all backslashes with \\\\: There is no backslash in code except in the f-string? Actually we have f\"Missing {key} in call\" which uses curly braces, no backslash. Also we have key=lambda ... no backslash. So no backslashes.\n- Replace all double quotes with \\\": There are many double quotes, e.g., \"Missing required fields\", 'tokens', etc. We'll need to escape each \" to \\\".\n- Replace all newlines with \\n.\n\nLet's produce the escaped string.\n\nI'll write it line by line, escaping.\n\nFirst line: \"def solve(case):\\n\"\nBut we need to include the whole code as a single string. I'll write it in a compact form.\n\nBetter approach: Use a Python script to generate the JSON. But since I'm an AI, I can simulate.\n\nLet me construct the JSON manually:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'tokens' 